## 1. Tell the DWP about your health

If you already get Universal Credit, report a **change of circumstances** in your online account, or tell your work coach. If you are claiming for the first time, say you have a health condition or disability when you apply.

## 2. Send a fit note

If you have been unwell for more than 7 days, you need a **fit note**. Upload it to your journal or give it to your work coach. Keep getting new fit notes until you have your decision. The **3 month waiting period** usually starts from the date you give the DWP medical evidence.

## 3. Fill in the UC50 form

The DWP sends you a **UC50** form, "Capability for work questionnaire". You usually have **4 weeks** to send it back. Describe your worst days as well as your better ones, and include letters from your GP, consultant or care team.

## 4. Have a Work Capability Assessment

Most people then have an assessment with a health professional, by phone, video or face to face. Read [the Work Capability Assessment explained](/benefits/esa/work-capability-assessment/) for what to expect.

If you are **terminally ill**, tell the DWP and ask your doctor or nurse for an **SR1** form. You will not need an assessment and there is no waiting period.

## 5. Get your decision

- **LCWRA:** you get the health element. Most new claimants get the lower rate. You get the higher rate if you meet the **severe conditions criteria** or are terminally ill.
- **LCW:** no extra money for most people, but fewer work rules and a work allowance.
- **Fit for work:** no health element.

If you disagree, ask for a **mandatory reconsideration** within **one month**, then you can appeal to a tribunal.

## Also claiming New Style ESA?

If you have paid enough National Insurance, you may be able to get [New Style ESA](/benefits/esa/) too. One Work Capability Assessment is used for both.

## Free help

Advisers at Citizens Advice, Turn2us and local welfare rights services can help with forms and challenges for free.
